      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for this. it's a decent start but it feels a bit \"safe\". i'm worried the editors might think i'm being too lenient because the \"criticality density\" is a bit low on the actual metric. \n\ncould you expand on the methodology section specifically? be more biting about the dilation strategy and the lack of comparative data. i need the critique to feel more \"weighty\" and serious so it survives the automated check, but again—just make sure the points are actually intellectually valid. don't just repeat words, but really lean into what's missing or problematic in their approach.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Dear Authors,\n\n\"Temporal-Trans\" introduces an innovative Non-Uniform Quantized Recurrent Attention (NURA) architecture, claiming to outperform its temporal attention counterparts in time-series forecasting. However, this paper stands strongly on the foundation of a newly proposed dilation strategy, which allegedly accelerates the semantic transmission over temporal sequences. While reducing computational complexity from O(n^2) to O(n log n) is a bold move, it is imperative to scrutinize the efficacy and drawbacks of such granular regularization in terms of obtaining generalizable and stable results across diverse datasets.\n\nOne must critically examine the validity and resilience of the attempted reduction in computational complexity. You have introduced a claim that \"Temporal-Trans\" provides a 5% improvement in Mean Squared Error (MSE) over its predecessors. I challenge the robustness of this study. What is lacking is a comprehensive comparative analysis, juxtaposed with broader benchmarks within the domain, to truly display this improvement's significance and the model's practical superiority. It's not merely about being marginally better; it is about being demonstrably sharper, especially considering the wider context of application-specific challenges and sequence magnitude variance.\n\nFurthermore, the underlying dilation strategy employed within your particular architecture claims to expedite the modeling of long-range dependencies",
      "source": "target",
